---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
const href = (route: string) => `${base}${route.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
const workflow = 'https://maxwell3919.github.io/DFT-Research-Workflow/operations/';
---

<BaseLayout title="Computational Tools" description="A selective map from computational implementations to their scientific objects." current="computational-tools">
  <h1>Computational Tools</h1>
  <p class="lede">Tools implement methods, representations, data models, or provenance. Availability of a feature does not establish its suitability for a material or observable.</p>

  <h2>Electronic-structure engines</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="https://www.quantum-espresso.org/">Quantum ESPRESSO</a></dt>
    <dd>An open plane-wave/pseudopotential suite for periodic DFT, perturbation theory, and related methods. Use it when that representation and its versioned datasets fit the model; connect <a href={href('/theory/plane-wave-and-real-space-methods/')}>plane waves</a> to <a href={`${workflow}choose-dft-method-and-computational-setup/`}>setup choice</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://fhi-aims.org/">FHI-aims</a></dt>
    <dd>An all-electron code based on numeric atom-centred orbitals for molecules and solids. It exposes localized-basis and core-explicit choices; basis tiers, grids, access terms, and release remain calculation inputs. See <a href={href('/theory/localized-orbital-methods/')}>localized orbitals</a> and <a href={`${workflow}test-numerical-convergence/`}>observable convergence</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://pyscf.org/">PySCF</a></dt>
    <dd>An open Python framework for molecular and periodic Hartree–Fock, DFT, correlated wavefunction, response, and embedding methods. It is useful for explicit method construction, but basis, reference stability, active space, and boundary model remain scientific decisions. See <a href={href('/theory/hartree-and-hartree-fock-theory/')}>Hartree–Fock</a> and <a href={`${workflow}calculate-reference-ground-state/`}>reference states</a>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Structure, symmetry, and visualization</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="https://ase-lib.org/">Atomic Simulation Environment</a></dt>
    <dd>An open Python layer for constructing structures and connecting atomistic calculators. It is useful for model manipulation and automation, not independent validation of the attached calculator. See <a href={href('/theory/crystallography/')}>crystallography</a> and <a href={`${workflow}build-or-modify-computational-model/`}>model construction</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://spglib.readthedocs.io/">Spglib</a></dt>
    <dd>An open crystal-symmetry library. It identifies symmetry under declared structural and magnetic tolerances; it does not decide whether that symmetry should constrain the physical model. See <a href={href('/theory/group-theory-and-symmetry/')}>symmetry</a> and <a href={`${workflow}obtain-material-structure/`}>structure provenance</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://jp-minerals.org/vesta/en/">VESTA</a></dt>
    <dd>A desktop viewer for crystal structures and volumetric data, distributed under its official use and redistribution terms. It is useful for inspection; a rendering does not validate a structure, density partition, or bond. See <a href={href('/theory/chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure/')}>bonding interpretations</a> and <a href={`${workflow}build-or-modify-computational-model/`}>model construction</a>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Materials databases</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="https://www.crystallography.net/cod/">Crystallography Open Database</a></dt>
    <dd>An open collection of experimentally reported crystal structures with a public-domain data policy. Use it to locate candidate structures, then inspect measurement context, disorder, occupancy, and model suitability. Connect <a href={href('/theory/crystallography/')}>crystallographic identity</a> to <a href={`${workflow}obtain-material-structure/`}>structure acquisition</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://nomad-lab.eu/">NOMAD</a></dt>
    <dd>A materials-data platform with parsers, APIs, metadata, and publication services. Use it to discover calculations and provenance; parser success does not establish numerical or physical adequacy. Compare the declared <a href={href('/methods/')}>method object</a> before <a href={`${workflow}validate-results-and-scientific-conclusions/`}>validation</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://www.optimade.org/">OPTIMADE</a></dt>
    <dd>A specification for interoperable queries across materials databases. It normalizes access to fields, not the source databases’ methods, corrections, releases, or uncertainties. Treat returned <a href={href('/theory/crystallography/')}>structures</a> as sourced model candidates, then follow <a href={`${workflow}obtain-material-structure/`}>structure acquisition</a>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Phonon, Wannier, and response ecosystem</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="https://phonopy.github.io/phonopy/">phonopy</a></dt>
    <dd>An open finite-displacement implementation for harmonic and quasi-harmonic lattice dynamics. It turns forces into force constants and phonon objects; supercell, displacement, force, q-space, and non-analytic choices remain observable-specific. See <a href={href('/theory/classical-mechanics/')}>normal modes</a> and <a href={`${workflow}harmonic-phonons/`}>phonon execution</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://wannier.org/">Wannier90</a></dt>
    <dd>An open implementation of maximally localized Wannier functions and related interpolation. Use it for a declared band subspace; windows, gauge, localization, topology obstructions, and matrix-element fidelity must be checked. See <a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry and topology</a> and <a href={`${workflow}wannier-function-construction/`}>Wannier construction</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://docs.epw-code.org/">EPW</a></dt>
    <dd>An open DFPT and Wannier–Fourier implementation for electron–phonon quantities. It is useful for dense interpolation of coupling objects, but mesh, interpolation, screening, and final-observable convergence remain distinct. Connect <a href={href('/theory/linear-response-and-excited-states/')}>response objects</a> to <a href={`${workflow}electron-phonon-coupling/`}>electron–phonon calculations</a>.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://berkeleygw.org/documentation/">BerkeleyGW</a></dt>
    <dd>An open GW and GW–BSE implementation for quasiparticle and neutral optical objects; official documentation identifies versioned manuals and examples. Starting point, screening, empty-state/frequency representation, Coulomb boundary, and kernel convergence remain model decisions. See <a href={href('/theory/many-body-perturbation-theory-and-quasiparticles/')}>many-body perturbation theory</a> and <a href={`${workflow}quasiparticle-corrections/`}>quasiparticle calculations</a>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Workflow and provenance infrastructure</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="https://www.aiida.net/">AiiDA</a></dt>
    <dd>An open workflow system that records process inputs, outputs, and lineage in a provenance graph. Use it to automate and preserve declared <a href={href('/methods/')}>method objects</a>; provenance does not certify convergence or scientific support. DFT Research Workflow defines the corresponding <a href={`${workflow}document-and-preserve-study/`}>study record</a>.</dd>
  </dl>
</BaseLayout>
